<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;"><em>PM request. Placeholder.</em></span></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Never thought that the GOC could declare war on 85% of the Milky Way Galaxy, but man were we wrong.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Immediately, you have my attention, but I'm not sure if it's in a good way or bad way.</p>
<p>Generally, a first SCP shouldn't be too ambitious or grand, as all that ambition can come crashing right back down on you.</p>
<p>I do speak from experience.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX/2 are monoliths that contains SCP-XXXX/3 which is gravitonium, SCP-XXXX/2 is made of an unknown element never seen before but are considered safe, SCP-XXXX/3 on the other hand is highly dangerous and is considered unpredictable, SCP-XXXX/3 has the ability to manipulate gravity which can result in casualties.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At first, I was a bit confused by the wording; I thought SCP-XXXX-2 (that's the proper formatting btw) was made of SCP-XXXX-3, but then it occurred to me that it was just the container.</p>
<p>Overall, you got some interesting bits, but it feels like the start to a generic sci-fi/science fantasy space war story.</p>
<p>I don't really see why the GoC would just declare war like that on 85% of the milky way galaxy? If anything, I see the orca-humanoids declaring war on the GoC since they blew up their ship.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation pacifically one of the O5 counsel could gain a seat in the galactic senate allowing the foundation to have an influence on the galaxy.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Foundation lore is entirely up to headcanon since no Canon exists, and this may be just me, but I'm not sure if people would buy having the Foundation not already be well versed in galactic level anomalies happening; there are plenty of other interstellar SCPs and you'd have to think of some damn fine reasoning for why they'd want to team up with some random new orca-humanoids.</p>
<p>If anything, I see them confiscating the gravitonium and containing the survivors.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The second being the Capital Planet which have a strong government, a strong military and controls 85% of the entire galaxy, The Capital Planet could be a new Group of Interest sense they share some of the same goals, to protect humanity even though the Capital actually protects all underdeveloped civilizations,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This definitely feels like generic sci-fi world building.</p>
<p>Granted, I admit I'm not the best person to seek out for sci-fi critique.</p>
<p>All in all, I'm sorry, but I'm not feeling this. It feels like generic sci-fi, space fantasy space warfare and it also feels way too ambitious for a first SCP, as weird of a critique as that may be.</p>
