			<div class="content" id="post-content-4850927">
									

<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there EnderDragon101. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<p>Overall, I agree with <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6391595&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608645818" alt="Quadraginta" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6391595)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;">Quadraginta</a></span>’s assessment: the concept sounds too ambitious, with you including multiple anomalies with little to no explanation as to their purpose or intention.</p>
<p>Mind you, it’s not about whether the concept is heavily sci-fi or not. It’s what you do with the many elements you’ve cited. What does the GOC have to do shooting down an alien ship, for example? What does “Galactic.Co” have to do with the alien’s purpose for being on earth? Why cross a human with an orca to make the alien? Is it just to make them look/sound cool? Finally, the whole description of SCP-xxxx-2 and SCP-xxxx-3 is overly confusing. It’s not clear <em>why</em> you needed to have two different materials in the first place, and what their significance is to the Foundation or the GOC.</p>
<p>The danger of picking a sci-fi setting is most beginning authors feel that they can get away with including multiple out-of-this-world / bizarre concepts and then make the excuse that “Oh, it’s just because it’s sci-fi” for their existence. Sorry, but the base rules of storytelling still apply: tell us what’s happening in your Central Narrative segment, and ensure that all the elements are clearly linked together. A majority of your Central Narrative atm is simply describing the SCP, and not going into enough detail about the actual narrative.</p>
<p>I hope you continue working on the concept though. The strength of a sci-fi concept is that you can really think out of the box, but some restraint is needed here. If you decide to revise the concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll take another look. Best of luck!</p>

							</div>

								
					
